= Entropy =

== Overview ==
'''Entropy''' is a family of quantitative concepts used to measure multiplicity, uncertainty, or dispersal under specific mathematical or physical formalisms.

Two major uses are thermodynamic/statistical-mechanical entropy and Shannon entropy in [[Information theory]].

They are mathematically related but should not be treated as one identical physical quantity.

== Statistical and thermodynamic entropy ==
In statistical mechanics, entropy is related to the number or probability distribution of microscopic states compatible with macroscopic conditions.

A Boltzmann form is:

<pre>
S = k_B ln W
</pre>

A Gibbs form is:

<pre>
S = -k_B Σ_i p_i ln p_i
</pre>

== Shannon entropy ==
For a discrete random variable X:

<pre>
H(X) = -Σ_x p(x) log p(x)
</pre>

It measures average uncertainty or average self-information.

The log base determines units such as bits or nats.

== Entropy and disorder ==
“Disorder” is a common intuition but not a universal definition.

Entropy can increase or decrease in situations where informal visual “disorder” is misleading.

Precise interpretation depends on the formalism and state space.

== Relationship between physical and informational entropy ==
Shannon and Gibbs entropies share the same logarithmic probability structure.

Their relationship is deep and useful.

However:

<pre>
Shannon entropy ≠ thermodynamic entropy by identity
</pre>

Physical entropy includes physical state definitions and units such as Boltzmann's constant.

